<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Ghazali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1058–<a href="/years/1111">1111</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Algazelus, Abū Ḥāmid Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad Ghazālī Ṭūsi, Ḥujjat al-Islām</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Ghazali was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> Ash'ari <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim scholar and polymath, one of the most influential jurists, philosophers, theologians and mystics in Islamic history. Born about 1058 in Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, he became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, but after a spiritual crisis in 1095 he left Baghdad and spent some ten years away from his post, when many of his major works were written, among them The Revival of the Religious Sciences. His The Incoherence of the Philosophers advanced a critique of Aristotelian science. Sunni Muslims consider him the mujaddid of the 11th century and call him the &quot;Proof of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>&quot;. He returned to teach at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in 1106 and died in 1111.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Ghazali was born about 1058 in Tabaran, a town in the district of Tus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, of Persian Muslim descent. His contemporary and first biographer, Abd al-Ghafir al-Farsi, records only that he began instruction in jurisprudence with the local teacher Ahmad al-Radhakani and with Abu Ali Farmadi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> from Tus. He later studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> under the jurist and theologian al-Juwayni, perhaps after a period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a>. After al-Juwayni's death in 1085 he joined the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, the powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a> of law and the Asharite school of theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1095, at the peak of his career as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, then the most prestigious academic position in the Muslim world, he stepped down. He was said to suffer emotional and physical difficulties, including loss of appetite and trouble lecturing; physicians who could not cure him judged the cause spiritual. He was critical of his institution, his peers and the political class, and he gave out his property, at first saying he was going on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> so that leaders could not prevent him.</p><p>The Incoherence of the Philosophers took aim at the philosophers of the 8th to 11th centuries, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and al-Farabi. In it he argued that causal events are the immediate will of God; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> burns on contact with fire only because God wills it. In the next century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a> (Averroes) answered with The Incoherence of the Incoherence. The Revival of the Religious Sciences, in four sections on worship, daily life, the ways to perdition and the ways to salvation, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> theology and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mysticism together; it was burned in Almoravid Spain in 1109 and 1143. The Alchemy of Happiness is a shorter Persian version of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Ghazali died on 19 December 1111. He mentioned &quot;more than 70&quot; works in a letter to Sultan Sanjar, and some five dozen are plausibly identified, while several hundred attributed works are doubtful or spurious. In his Fada'ih al-Batiniyya he declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a> unbelievers whose blood may be spilled, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> was assassinated by Ismailis. Sunni Muslims consider him the mujaddid, or renewer, of the faith of the 11th century and call him the &quot;Proof of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>&quot;. The scholar Nawawi said that if all the books of Islam were lost but the Ihya', it would suffice to replace them. His writings circulated among Christian and Jewish scholars as well, and Western thinkers influenced by him include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, Dante and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Ghazali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
